# Task Files (one per plan)

`context/tasks/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug-plan>.md` holds the task list for a
**plan**, with one `## Phase <n>` section per phase. One file per plan is the
default so a sequentially executed plan does not multiply files; split into
several files only to divide the work by context or across sub-agents (disjoint
scopes, each with a `--stream <label>` suffix). The task file is the
**execution unit**: work happens from one task file — and one phase section — at
a time, never from the plan.

## Create

Create the plan's task file with the CLI (scaffolds the path and frontmatter):

```bash
sdt context task add "<step>" [--phase <n>] [--stream <label>] [--plan <plan-file>]
```

`--phase <n>` places the step in the `## Phase <n>` section (created when
absent); without it the step lands in the file's unphased checklist. `--stream`
selects an opt-in split file (`<slug-plan>-<label>.md`). Timestamps
(`created`/`updated`) come from `sdt time iso` (RFC3339 UTC). Or hand-write the
file from the template below.

## Intent first

Task files follow the plan, which follows the analysis; the chain and its
approval gates are defined in AGENTS.md (HARD RULE 1).

A task file is owned by its plan: it carries the optional `phases` list (the
phase sections it covers; omitted on the whole-plan file) and **inherits the
plan's `objective`** for grouping — it never declares `objective` itself.

## Structure

```markdown
---
kind: tasks
summary: "<1-2 sentence summary — MANDATORY, index source>"
phases: [1, 2]           # optional: only on a split file; omitted on the whole-plan file
status: pending          # pending | in-progress | completed | archived | active(legacy) — statuses defined in the matrix (architecture/stack.md)
created: "<ISO 8601>"
updated: "<ISO 8601>"
links:
  - plan/<date>-<plan-slug>.md
sources:
  - plan/<date>-<plan-slug>.md
project: <project>
agent: <agent/tool>          # optional
model: <model id>            # optional
session: <session id>        # optional
---

## Design

- <analysis objective this task covers> — approach: <how>;
  verify: <command or signal>
- <analysis objective this task covers> — approach: <how>;
  verify: <command or signal>

## Phase 1 — <goal>

- [ ] step one <!-- c1 -->
- [~] step two (in progress) <!-- c2 -->

## Phase 2 — <goal>

- [x] step three (done) <!-- c3 -->
- [!] step four (blocked) <!-- c4 -->

## Review                 <!-- file-level; written by `task review` on completion -->
```

The trailing `<!-- c<N> -->` is a stable per-document id the CLI assigns; never
type or renumber it by hand.

## Design section (study of the task objectives)

Before executing, add a `## Design` section: one short block per objective the
task's phases cover. Each block names the objective (from the plan phase and the
analysis it derives from), the approach/design chosen for it, and how it will be
verified. Keep it short — it studies *this task's* objectives; the full style,
architecture and dependency contract lives in the plan (see
`instructions/plan.md`), so do not duplicate it here.

## Checklist item detail

Each checklist item should be a single, self-contained action. For items that
need more context, use a description indented below the checkbox:

```markdown
- [ ] Refactor auth middleware to use token pool
      File: `internal/auth/middleware.go` — replace per-request
      token allocation with a sync.Pool; must not change the public API.
- [~] Add unit tests for token pool
      Must cover: concurrent access, pool exhaustion fallback.
      Verification: `go test ./internal/auth/ -race`
- [ ] Update migration script
      Depends on: refactor auth middleware.
```

Keep descriptions concise: what changes, which files, what to verify. Avoid
full paragraphs — the task file's Verification commands and Definition of Done
sections handle that.

Each item gets a trailing `<!-- c<N> -->` id when the CLI writes it; the anchor
is invisible when rendered and lets `sdt context check`/`task <verb>` address the
item without depending on its position. Do not add, edit or reorder anchors by
hand.

## Frontmatter properties

| Property | Mandatory | Description |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| kind | yes | Document type, fixed value `tasks`; index and lint depend on it. |
| summary | yes | 1-2 sentence summary → index source. |
| phases | no | Phase sections this file covers (e.g. `[1, 2]`); only on a split file, omitted on the whole-plan file. The legacy scalar `phase` is still accepted. The task inherits the plan's `objective`; it does not carry one. |
| status | yes | File state: `pending` (to work on) / `in-progress` / `completed` / `archived`. The first three are always distinguishable — a task file is never stuck with fewer than three meaningful states. `active` is the legacy value accepted by lint. |
| created | yes | ISO 8601 UTC creation date (use `sdt time iso`). |
| updated | yes | ISO 8601 UTC last-edit date, written only by the CLI; refresh a body-only edit with `sdt context touch`. |
| links | yes | Relative path to the plan this task file executes. |
| project | yes | Project id from `.sdt.yaml` (e.g. `sdt_44f24890`). |
| agent | no | Agent/tool that picked up or last edited the file. |
| model | no | Model id of that agent — provenance. |
| session | no | Session id for traceability across edits. |

## Rules

- **No H1 title** — body starts at H2; see AGENTS.md (document conventions).
- **CLI-only state** — never hand-edit `status:`, `updated:` or a checkbox
  marker. Mutate them with the CLI: `sdt context task <sub> --phase <n>`
  (add/list/done/block/wip/review) or, for any checklist, `sdt context check
  <doc> <id> --done|--wip|--block|--pending`; refresh a body-only edit with
  `sdt context touch <doc>`.
- Status markers: `[ ]` todo · `[~]` in-progress · `[x]` done · `[!]` blocked.
- File status transitions: create → `pending`; `wip`/`block` → `in-progress`; `done` → `completed` when no `[ ]`/`[~]` item remains, else `in-progress`. Completion is `completed` and the file stays in `context/tasks/`; `archived` is reserved for a task file retired by the user's will.
- **One file per plan by default**: the file holds the plan's phases as `## Phase
  <n>` sections. Split into several files only to divide the work by context or
  across sub-agents — disjoint scopes, each named with a `--stream <label>` and
  declaring its `phases` list. A plan phase with no matching section in any of
  its task files gets a `sdt context lint` SUGGESTION.
- Keep each phase section small (~5-7 items); a >10-item section triggers a
  `sdt context lint` SUGGESTION to split the phase.
- Task files are **living**: updated during execution of the phase.
- When every phase section is done and the `## Review` block is present, set the
  file `completed` (it stays in `context/tasks/`) and update the plan.

## Execution workflow

1. **Pick** — choose a task file whose dependencies are satisfied (see its plan
   and links). One file at a time; don't jump between files. Concurrent
   sub-agents must own different files (disjoint scopes).
2. **Take in charge** — before any work, mark the phase in progress with
   `sdt context task wip <id> --phase <n>` (it sets the item `[~]`, flips the
   file `in-progress` and refreshes `updated`; add `agent`/`model`/`session`
   when available). Study the task's objectives in the `## Design` section (fill
   it from the plan and analysis if missing). Log the take-in in the worklog.
3. **Execute** — work the phase section's items top to bottom; update markers as
   you go. Any deviation goes back into this file or the plan — never silently.
4. **Complete** — `[x]` every item of the phase, run the **verify-step** below,
   then move to the next phase; set the file `completed` (the `## Review` block
   is written file-level) and update the linked plan.
5. **Commit gate** — if the task produced tracked changes, propose a
   Conventional Commits message (see `instructions/git.md`) scoped to this
   task and **ask the user whether to commit**; commit only on explicit
   approval.

## Stale task files

At the start of any execution pass, run `sdt context status` (it flags
`in-progress` files) and scan `context/tasks/` for stale files: status
`in-progress` (or legacy `active`) with `[~]` items that went unupdated for a
long time (compare each file's `updated` with the current date and the recorded
take-in). For each stale file:

1. Receive it and adjust status: locate the exact point of work from
   `updated` and the `[~]` markers.
2. Verify against the linked plan and worklog whether work continued elsewhere
   or stalled.
3. If stalled: reset in-progress items to `[ ]` (todo) or close them
   `[x]` when actually finished; clear all `[~]`; refresh
   `updated`.
4. If the phase is finished but not closed: mark items `[x]`, set the file
   `completed`, update the plan, note it in the worklog.

Never leave `[~]` in-progress markers unattended across sessions, and never
archive a stale task file to hide it: `completed` (or `pending` after a reset)
is the normal terminal state, and archiving is reserved for a task file the user
explicitly retires.

## Verify-step

Run the verify-step before closing a phase (completeness, coherence,
correctness) and use the standardized claim vocabulary (**passed** / **expected**
/ **inferred**) — see AGENTS.md (5-stage development lifecycle).

Record it as a file-level `## Review` block in the task file (write it with
`sdt context task review --phase <n>`; a completed file without the block gets
a lint SUGGESTION). Every finding ends as one of the closed verdicts
**CONFIRMED**, **DISPROVED** or **UNVERIFIED**, with evidence (command output,
file path). Findings are validated in an **independent pass**; the author of the
phase does not self-approve.

Close-out checks, run before setting the file `completed`:

- `sdt context reindex` and `sdt context lint` — index regenerated, no new
  findings.
- `sdt context status` — no stale `in-progress` file left behind.
- `sdt context docs` — regenerate the per-command docs when the command help or
  the version changed (see `context/sdtdocs/README.md`).
